<script>
var CONTACT="info@followthroughguides.com",PUBLIC="https://claude.ai/artifact/UPPHnpugi29jcunfmSrimZ",FORM_URL="/api/playbook";
var CATS=["Trades & repair","Outdoor & hauling","Cleaning & detailing"];
var STEPS=[
["Form your LLC","Register an LLC with your state (usually $50–$500) to separate personal and business liability. Use your state’s Secretary of State website."],
["Get your EIN","Apply for a free Employer Identification Number on irs.gov. It takes about 10 minutes."],
["Open a business bank account","Open business checking with your LLC papers and EIN so business money stays separate from personal money."],
["Get insurance","Buy general liability insurance before your first job. Many customers ask for proof."],
["Set your prices","Work out your costs and a profit margin, then set clear flat rates or hourly prices."],
["Find your first customers","Start with people you know, a Google Business Profile, local Facebook groups and neighbors."],
["Handle objections","Expect questions about price, timing and trust. Prepare calm, honest answers in advance."]];
var T=[
{s:"hvac",full:1,n:"HVAC",c:0,cost:"$7,000–$50,000",season:"Summer and winter are busy, and heat waves and cold snaps bring emergency calls. Spring and fall are slower, so that’s when you sell tune-ups and maintenance plans.",fit:"Good if you like technical troubleshooting and can earn a state license and EPA 608 certification. The hard truth: most licensing states want years of documented experience first, a replacement ties up thousands in equipment before you’re paid, and peak weeks are long and hot.",spend:"Licensing and EPA 608, gauges, vacuum pump and recovery machine, a work van, insurance and workers’ comp, parts stock, scheduling software.",tips:["Check your state’s HVAC license before you form the LLC. In licensing states the business usually needs a licensed person tied to it (California calls this the qualifier), so set it up that way from the start.","Free at irs.gov.","Keep equipment money separate. One replacement can tie up a few thousand dollars before you’re paid, so pay the distributor from that job’s deposit, not from the next one.","Ask for completed-operations and pollution cover (refrigerant, mold from condensate leaks). California requires workers’ comp for HVAC licensees even with no employees.","Charge a diagnostic fee, price repairs from a flat-rate book, and quote replacements per system with equipment, labor, permit and warranty itemized.","Sell spring and fall tune-ups to neighbors, landlords and property managers, then turn them into yearly maintenance plans.","When someone asks “Can’t you just add refrigerant?”, explain that low refrigerant means a leak, and show the repair and replacement prices side by side."]},
{s:"plumbing",full:1,n:"Plumbing",c:0,cost:"$7,000–$55,000",season:"Year-round. Winter freezes burst pipes, and the days after Thanksgiving and other big holidays bring a rush of drain and sewer calls.",fit:"Good if you hold or are working toward a plumbing license and don’t mind emergencies, crawlspaces and sewage. The hard truth: most states require years as an apprentice and journeyman first, and one bad fitting can mean a water-damage claim.",spend:"Licensing and bond, drain machines, a press tool and sewer camera, a stocked van, insurance, parts and water heaters.",tips:["Check who must hold the license before you form the LLC. In Texas, for example, a plumbing company works under a responsible master plumber, who files proof of insurance with the state board.","Free at irs.gov.","Keep a separate budget for van stock and water heaters, so restocking never quietly eats a job’s profit.","Make sure your liability policy covers water damage you cause and work you’ve finished, and ask whether your city requires a bond.","Quote flat rates per task from a price book, with a trip fee and a higher after-hours rate.","Property managers and landlords call whoever answers first when a tenant has a leak. Give them a direct number and send photos from every job.","When someone says “It’s only a drip,” explain what a slow leak does under a cabinet or slab, and offer a flat price to fix it today."]},
{s:"roofing",full:1,n:"Roofing",c:0,cost:"$10,000–$40,000",season:"Spring through fall. Storm seasons spike demand.",fit:"Good if you’re comfortable with heights and physical work and can handle big quotes. Insurance and workers’ comp costs are high.",spend:"Insurance and workers’ comp, ladders and safety gear, tools, a truck and trailer, material accounts.",tips:["Some states require a roofing license. Check yours before you quote.","Free at irs.gov.","Keep deposits and material costs clearly tracked. Roofing runs on cash flow.","Workers’ comp matters here. Customers often ask for proof of both policies.","Price per square (100 sq ft), add waste, tear-off and cleanup.","Door knock after storms (where legal) and ask neighbors for referrals.","When someone says “I need more quotes,” offer to explain your materials and warranty."]},
{s:"painting",full:1,n:"Painting",c:0,cost:"$2,500–$35,000",season:"Exteriors run spring through fall and stop for rain and cold, since most exterior paints need dry days above roughly 35–50°F. Interiors work year-round and fill the winter.",fit:"A low-cost start for tidy, detail-minded people. The hard truth: the barrier to entry is low, so competition is fierce, prep is most of the work, and many homes built before 1978 need lead-safe certification.",spend:"Ladders and planks, an airless sprayer, brushes and rollers, drop cloths and plastic, EPA lead-safe certification, insurance, a vehicle, paint until customers pay.",tips:["Check whether your state licenses painters (California, Oregon and Arizona do). Form the LLC first if you’ll work on pre-1978 homes, because EPA lead-safe firm certification is issued to the business.","Free at irs.gov.","Run every paint store charge through the business account, so each job’s paint, tape and plastic show up against that job.","Make sure your policy covers damage to customers’ belongings and overspray on cars and neighbors’ homes, and price workers’ comp before your first hire.","Price by the square foot of wall or siding, not floor area, and add prep, ceilings, trim and doors separately.","Introduce yourself to realtors and property managers. Pre-listing touch-ups and rental turnovers are fast, repeat work.","If they say “My nephew can paint it,” explain prep, insurance and lead-safe rules on older homes, and offer a written warranty."]},
{s:"handyman",full:1,n:"Handyman",c:0,cost:"$2,000–$30,000",season:"Year-round, with peaks in spring (outdoor repairs and home sales) and fall (weatherproofing before winter). Late December and January are usually slow.",fit:"Great for people with broad skills who like variety and talking with customers. The hard truth: many states cap job size without a license, licensed trades like electrical and plumbing are off limits, and small jobs lose money to driving and supply runs unless you price for them.",spend:"Hand and power tools, a vehicle with storage, liability and business auto insurance, advertising, a small materials float.",tips:["Learn your state’s handyman limit before you register. In California and Arizona, for example, jobs of $1,000 or more (labor and materials) or jobs that need a permit require a contractor license.","Free at irs.gov.","Take card payments on your phone and get paid before you leave. On small jobs, chasing an invoice can cost more than the job earned.","General liability matters because you work inside people’s homes. Also tell your auto insurer you drive to jobs, since personal policies may not cover business use.","Set an hourly rate with a minimum (often 1–2 hours), and turn common jobs into flat prices on a service menu.","Introduce yourself to realtors and property managers. Inspection repair lists and rental turnovers are steady handyman work.","When someone says “That’s a lot for a small job,” explain that your minimum covers travel, tools and insurance, and offer to tackle their whole list in one visit."]},
{s:"landscaping",full:1,n:"Landscaping",c:1,cost:"$4,000–$55,000",season:"Spring through fall, with the biggest rush from April to June when grass grows fastest. Fall leaf cleanups are a second peak, and snow removal in the North or year-round mowing in the South can fill winter.",fit:"Good if you like outdoor physical work, can keep a weekly schedule and want recurring income. It’s easy to start, so price competition is stiff, and in most of the country income drops in winter.",spend:"Mowers, trimmers and blowers, a trailer, a truck, fuel and repairs, insurance, door hangers and software.",tips:["Mowing rarely needs a state license, but installs can. California, for example, requires a C-27 license for landscape jobs of $1,000 or more.","Free at irs.gov.","Bill weekly clients monthly on autopay, and save part of each summer month to carry you through the slow winter.","Get general liability, commercial auto for the truck and trailer, and cover for your mowers against theft.","Price per visit from time on site plus drive time, and set a minimum charge per stop.","Hang door hangers on the streets you already mow. Never put flyers in mailboxes, which breaks federal law.","If they say “my neighbor’s kid mows for $25,” point to insurance, clean edges and never missing a week."]},
{s:"junk-removal",full:1,n:"Junk Removal",c:1,cost:"$4,000–$60,000",season:"Year-round, busiest from spring cleaning through summer move-outs. The end of each month, college move-out in May and the weeks after the holidays spike calls, and estate clean-outs come in all year.",fit:"Good if you can lift heavy, awkward items all day and like jobs that pay the same day. It’s easy to start, but dump fees, fuel and truck repairs eat margins, and the work is hard on your body.",spend:"A truck or trailer, dump fees, fuel, commercial auto insurance, dollies and straps, ads and truck lettering.",tips:["Most states don’t license junk hauling, but some regulate anyone who hauls other people’s waste. New Jersey, for example, requires an A-901 license.","Free at irs.gov.","Log dump fees and fuel against each job so you see your real margin on every load.","A personal auto policy won’t cover a truck used for hauling. Get commercial auto as well as general liability.","Price by how much of the truck a load fills, plus set fees for mattresses, tires and appliances.","Pitch realtors, property managers and estate attorneys, who need fast clean-outs again and again.","If they say “I’ll just rent a dumpster,” compare the street permit, the wait and the lifting you save them."]},
{s:"hauling-transportation",full:1,n:"Hauling & Transportation",c:1,cost:"$3,000–$70,000",season:"Busiest from May through September, when most families move, and at the end of every month and on weekends. Winter is slower, so many movers discount weekday and off-season dates.",fit:"Good if you’re strong, organized and calm under deadlines, and can lead a small crew. Insurance and workers’ comp are expensive, damage claims come with the job, and crossing a state line brings federal rules.",spend:"Insurance and workers’ comp, helper wages, a box truck or rental, pads, dollies and straps, fuel, marketing.",tips:["Many states license local movers. California and Texas, for example, require a state permit or registration before you move anyone’s household goods.","Free at irs.gov.","Run helpers through payroll, not cash, and set aside money for payroll taxes and workers’ comp every week.","General liability usually excludes customers’ belongings. Add cargo or bailee cover, and commercial auto for the truck.","Price per crew-hour with a 2–3 hour minimum, and charge for travel time and materials.","Take labor-only jobs on HireAHelper or U-Haul’s Moving Help, and introduce yourself to realtors and apartment managers.","If they ask “why not just hire guys off Craigslist?”, explain insurance, payroll and who pays when something breaks."]},
{s:"house-cleaning",full:1,n:"House Cleaning",c:2,cost:"$1,500–$25,000",season:"Year-round, built on recurring visits. Spring cleaning, the weeks before the holidays and the summer moving season spike deep and move-out cleans, while vacations bring skipped visits.",fit:"Good if you’re reliable, detail-minded and fine with physical, repetitive work. Start-up costs are low, but so is the barrier to entry, so trust and consistency win clients, and margins get thin once you pay a team properly.",spend:"Liability insurance and a janitorial bond, a commercial vacuum and supplies, a car, booking software, and payroll and workers’ comp once you hire.",tips:["Most states don’t license house cleaners, but your city or county likely requires a business license. Get it before your first paid clean.","Free at irs.gov.","Charge clients through the business account and set aside sales tax where it applies. Texas, for example, taxes home cleaning sold by a cleaning business.","Carry general liability and a janitorial bond, which pays clients for theft by your employees. Clients and property managers often ask for proof of both.","Price per visit from the labor hours a home takes, based on bedrooms, bathrooms and square footage. Quote the first deep clean separately.","Post in neighborhood groups and on Nextdoor, and ask realtors and property managers for move-out cleans. Offer every one-time client a recurring slot.","When someone says “I’ll find someone cheaper,” ask what the other quote includes and send your room-by-room checklist."]},
{s:"car-detailing",full:1,n:"Car Detailing",c:2,cost:"$3,000–$35,000",season:"Spring through fall, with spring pollen, summer road trips and pre-winter sealant work as the peaks. Snow-belt winters slow down, and rain and high heat force reschedules.",fit:"Good if you love cars, have patience for detail and can work outdoors in the heat. Going mobile keeps start-up costs low, but a burned paint edge or stained seat is on you, so practice before you charge and carry garagekeepers coverage.",spend:"Liability and garagekeepers insurance, a polisher, extractor and pressure washer, a water tank and generator, chemicals and towels, a vehicle for your rig, booking software.",tips:["Detailing usually needs only a business license, but some cities (Fort Worth, for example) require a permit for mobile washing. Check yours first.","Free at irs.gov.","Track chemical and coating costs per car, and set aside sales tax if your state taxes detailing, as Connecticut and Washington do.","Ask for garagekeepers coverage. General liability usually excludes customers’ cars while they’re in your care.","Price per vehicle by size and condition, with set fees for pet hair, stains and heavy soil.","Post before-and-after photos and short videos, and pitch used-car dealers and fleet managers for repeat work.","When someone says “I’ll just take it through the car wash,” show them swirl marks in direct light and what a polished test spot looks like."]}
];
var OBJ=[["Roofing","“Your price is higher than the other guy’s.”","“That’s fair, and I’d compare too. What’s included in the other quote? Materials, warranty and cleanup are where prices differ, and I’d rather you choose on what you get.”"],["HVAC","“I need to talk to my spouse first.”","“Of course. What do you think they’ll want to know? I’ll write a one-page summary so you have it all in one place, and I’ll check back Thursday.”"],["House cleaning","“I can find someone cheaper.”","“You probably can. Most clients stay with us for reliability: same crew, on time, same result. Would a one-time deep clean be a good way to see the difference?”"]];
var LEGAL={
privacy:["Privacy policy","<p>Last updated: October 25, 2026. Contact: "+CONTACT+".</p><h3>What I collect</h3><p>Only what you send me, such as your email address when you request a playbook. This site has no accounts and no tracking or advertising cookies.</p><h3>Your browser storage</h3><p>Your checklist progress is saved in your own browser (local storage) so it is there when you return. It never leaves your device.</p><h3>Third parties</h3><p>Fonts load from Google Fonts, which may receive your IP address. When you request a playbook, your email address goes through this site’s server and its email delivery service (Brevo) to me.</p><h3>How I use it</h3><p>To send the playbook you asked for and occasional related tips. Every email includes an unsubscribe option, and I never sell your data. Legal basis (EU/UK): your consent, which you can withdraw anytime.</p><h3>Your rights</h3><p>You can ask to see, correct or delete your data, or object to its use, by emailing me. California and other US state residents have similar rights. I keep data only as long as needed.</p><h3>Children</h3><p>This site is for adults and is not directed at children under 16.</p><h3>Changes</h3><p>I may update this policy and will change the date above.</p>"],
terms:["Terms of use","<p>By using this site you agree to these terms.</p><h3>Information only</h3><p>Content is general education. It is provided “as is” without warranties of any kind.</p><h3>Intellectual property</h3><p>The text, design and playbooks belong to FollowThrough. You may use them for your own business but not resell or republish them.</p><h3>Limitation of liability</h3><p>To the fullest extent allowed by law, I am not liable for losses arising from your use of this site or reliance on its content.</p><h3>Links</h3><p>I am not responsible for third-party sites linked from here.</p><h3>Changes</h3><p>I may update these terms at any time.</p>"],
disclaimer:["Disclaimer","<h3>Not professional advice</h3><p>Nothing here is legal, tax, financial, insurance or licensing advice. Laws and requirements vary by state and city, so check with your state agency and a qualified professional before acting.</p><h3>Cost and income figures</h3><p>Start-up costs and seasons are rough estimates and vary by location and scale. I make no promise of income or results.</p><h3>No affiliation</h3><p>This site is independent and is not affiliated with any government agency, insurer or company mentioned. Some links may earn me a commission; see the <a href=\"#affiliate\">affiliate disclosure</a>.</p><h3>Communications</h3><p>I send emails only to people who ask for them, and you can opt out at any time.</p>"],
affiliate:["Affiliate disclosure","<p>Last updated: October 25, 2026. Contact: "+CONTACT+".</p><h3>What this means</h3><p>Some links on this site may be affiliate links. If you click one and then buy or sign up, I may earn a commission from the company, at no extra cost to you.</p><h3>Which links</h3><p>Affiliate links point to products and services for running a service business, such as quoting, scheduling, invoicing and payroll software. Links to government agencies, licensing boards and official forms are never affiliate links.</p><h3>How I choose what to mention</h3><p>I mention a product because I think it’s worth considering for a new service business, not because it pays me. Companies can’t pay to be included or to change what a guide says, and guides also mention products that pay nothing. Compare the options and choose what fits your business.</p><h3>How you’ll know</h3><p>Where a page has affiliate links, it says so near the links, as well as here.</p><h3>Changes</h3><p>I may update this disclosure and will change the date above.</p>"]};
var L=function(a){return "<ul>"+a.map(function(x){return "<li>"+x+"</li>"}).join("")+"</ul>"};
